node_modules/
dist/
build/
coverage/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo

# A pnpm store inside the repository. `pnpm install --store-dir .pnpm-store` puts one
# here, which is a reasonable thing to do on a machine with several checkouts — it is
# 19 MB of content-addressed tarballs that nothing builds from, and `git add -A` would
# otherwise commit every one of them.
.pnpm-store/

.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
!.env.example

# Editor and IDE state. Deliberately not committed: several agents work
# in different tools, and a shared .idea/ or .vscode/ means every session
# produces a diff that has nothing to do with the code.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# Claude Code's per-machine state, inside a directory that is otherwise committed.
#
# `.claude/launch.json` is shared on purpose — it is how `pnpm --filter @flux/web dev`
# gets started for a preview, and it is the same command on every machine. The two
# files below are not: `settings.local.json` is the permission allow-list a session
# accumulates, which is specific to one person's machine and one person's risk
# appetite, and `memory/` is a session's own notes. Neither is a fact about the
# product. This repository is public, so the cost of a `git add -A` sweeping either
# one in is higher than a stray diff.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/memory/

playwright-report/
test-results/
.vite/

# UI reference material, kept locally and out of history on purpose.
#
# These are the screenshots and the screen recording the UI is designed against.
# `JIRA 5.mp4` alone is 11 MB, and git stores every version of a binary forever —
# one re-record and the clone is 22 MB heavier permanently, for a file nothing
# builds from. They are read by whoever is designing a surface, which is a local
# activity.
#
# If a reference ever needs to be shared, the right move is a compressed still
# committed next to the spec that cites it, not the source recording.
UI Images/
